Q: Create a Clickable Folder Link in Powershell A: <p>This is a quite simple way you could use to select a folder an open it, it will display an <code>Out-GridView</code> until you cancel or close the grid and open the folder once you make a selection. If you're looking for something <em>better looking</em> and more complex you would have to code your own UI using WinForms or WPF, etc.</p>
<pre><code>$directories = Get-ChildItem $env:USERPROFILE -Directory |
    Select-Object Name, FullName

while ($true) {
    $selection = $directories | Out-GridView -PassThru -Title 'Choose a Folder'
    if (-not $selection) { break }
    $selection | Invoke-Item -LiteralPath { $_.FullName }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Possible conversion loss of data (warning C4244) A: <p>There's nothing wrong with the compiler in this case</p>
<pre><code>ctx-&gt;data[63] = ctx-&gt;bit_len; // LINE 156 IS HERE!
ctx-&gt;data[62] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 8;
ctx-&gt;data[61] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 16;
ctx-&gt;data[60] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 24;
ctx-&gt;data[59] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 32;
ctx-&gt;data[58] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 40;
ctx-&gt;data[57] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 48;
ctx-&gt;data[56] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 56; // LINE 163 IS HERE!
</code></pre>
<p><code>ctx-&gt;bit_len</code> is a 64-bit type so obviously storing it into a <code>uint8_t</code> doesn't work. Notice there's no warning in the <code>digest[i]      = (ctx-&gt;state[0] &gt;&gt; (24 - i * 8)) &amp; 0x000000ff</code> lines, because there are proper masking with <code>0xFF</code> in those lines. The only case where it's lacking analysis is <code>ctx-&gt;data[56] = ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 56</code> because the result fits in a <code>uint8_t</code>, but it's still not wrong. To get the LSB you need to do an explicit cast so that the compiler knows what you're doing</p>
<pre><code>ctx-&gt;data[62] = uint8_t(ctx-&gt;bit_len &gt;&gt; 8);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>That said, there's no need to copy manually like that, just use <code>memcpy</code> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winsock2/nf-winsock2-ntohll" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ntohll</code></a>. The code isn't good and using an ancient compiler is even worse. There are lots of good crypto libraries nowadays that use proper <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_instruction,_multiple_data" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SIMD</a> and multithreading like OpenSSL, making them significantly fast</p>
